\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two fibers for binary tetrahedral symmetry}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take the [binary tetrahedral group](fdip-007G) and a fixed
finite substrate, with trivial substrate action in the
[representation datum](fdip-007H). Its spinor datum uses
#{\mathbb K=\mathbb C}, #{F=\mathbb C^2} with its standard
Hermitian metric, and the inclusion #{2T\subset SU(2)}.
Its rotation datum uses #{\mathbb K=\mathbb R}, #{F=\mathbb R^3}
with its Euclidean metric, and the representation obtained through
#{\pi:SU(2)\to SO(3)}.}

\p{The latter kills #{\{1,-1\}}, while the former is faithful.
Thus these choices define different fibers and observables even
though the abstract group and the substrate are the same. The
[spinor Wilson calculation](fdip-006Y) uses the former. A choice of
#{G} alone would not select that representation.}
